/**
@page api_beginner_guide Beginner Guide

# Read this first

The Nocturne C++ API is intended to answer two beginner questions before it shows
you member signatures:

1. **What is this type responsible for?**
2. **Should I call this function myself, and what lifetime/ownership rules apply?**

If a class page contains a method you do not recognize, do not infer usage from the
name alone. Read the class-level **When to use**, **Do not use for**,
**Ownership/lifetime**, **Threading** and **Typical flow** sections first.

# Recommended learning order

## 1. Runtime

Start with:

- @ref noc::Engine
- @ref noc::MainLoop
- @ref noc::EngineConfig

Understand the frame sequence before reading lower-level systems:

@code
Engine::BeginFrame()
    -> optional editor pre-Tick callback
    -> Engine::Tick()
    -> Engine::EndFrame()
@endcode

Important: @ref noc::Engine::TickOnce() is currently a diagnostic/smoke helper,
not one full game/editor frame.

## 2. World and ECS

Then read:

- @ref noc::EntityHandle
- @ref noc::EntityRegistry
- @ref noc::World
- @ref noc::TransformComponent
- @ref noc::RenderableComponent
- @ref noc::CameraComponent
- @ref noc::NameComponent

Remember:

@code
EntityHandle::IsValid()
@endcode

only checks the handle's local sentinel values. Use @ref noc::World::IsAlive()
when you need to know whether the entity is still alive.

## 3. Resources

Read:

- @ref noc::VirtualFileSystem
- @ref noc::ResourceHandle
- @ref noc::ResourceHandleT
- @ref noc::ResourceManager

The normal asynchronous idea is:

@code
request -> Loading
        -> worker I/O/decode
        -> ResourceManager::Update()
        -> Ready or Failed
@endcode

Do not assume a valid resource handle is ready.

## 4. Rendering

Read:

- @ref noc::RenderQueue
- @ref noc::RenderSystem
- @ref noc::Dx12Renderer

The important architecture boundary is:

@code
World / ECS
    -> BuildRenderQueue(frameArena, viewport)
    -> RenderQueue
    -> RenderSystem
    -> Dx12Renderer
@endcode

Most application/editor code should stop at RenderSystem. Dx12Renderer is backend
implementation detail.

## 5. Editor

Read:

- @ref nocturne::editor::EditorShellV3
- @ref nocturne::editor::EditorViewportController
- @ref nocturne::editor::EditorTheme

EditorShellV3 owns presentation/chrome.
EditorViewportController owns editor-only 3D interaction.
World remains authoritative for entity/component state.

# Ownership vocabulary used in these docs

**Owns**
: The object is responsible for lifetime/destruction of the referenced state.

**Borrowed**
: You may use the reference/pointer while the owner and documented lifetime remain
  valid. You must not delete/free it.

**Same-frame**
: The data may be invalid after the next Engine::BeginFrame(), especially when it
  comes from Engine::FrameArena().

**Structural mutation**
: Adding/removing/destroying entities/components. Dense ECS storage may relocate
  component objects, so borrowed component pointers should not be retained across it.

# Book grounding

The architecture explanations in these API comments follow the canonical Nocturne
system documents, which are grounded in:

- Jason Gregory, Game Engine Architecture (3rd ed.), §7.2 and §7.2.2 for runtime
  resource management;
- Gregory §8.2 for the master game loop;
- Gregory §8.6 for job/task decomposition;
- Gregory §11.2 and §11.2.1 for rendering-pipeline separation;
- Gregory §15.4 and §15.4.1 for world-editor responsibilities;
- Gregory §16.2.1.6, §16.2.2 and §16.5 for component/world/reference architecture;
- Bob Nystrom, Game Programming Patterns, Component and Data Locality;
- Frank Luna and Eric Lengyel for camera/projection fundamentals used by
  CameraComponent.

The exact Nocturne C++ API, Doxygen layout, handle formats, threading boundaries,
copy-for-AI surface and beginner-oriented wording are **Design choice (not directly
from the book)**.

# Where conceptual documentation fits

Doxygen explains symbol-level use. For architecture/rationale, follow the canonical
website documentation:

- Runtime: /docs/systems/runtime/
- Resources: /docs/systems/resources/
- Rendering: /docs/systems/rendering/
- World & ECS: /docs/systems/world-ecs/
- Editor: /docs/systems/editor/

Do not treat the existence of a private/backend type as an instruction that normal
application code should call it.
*/
